const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Development editing","serviceType":"Editing service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/academic-editorial-services/development-editing-service/","description":"Developmental editing for manuscripts that are sound but not yet readable as an argument. Enhance clarity and improve your manuscript with expert development.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How is development editing different from copyediting?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Development editing works on content, structure, argumentation, clarity and consistency. It may reorganise sections, refine ideas and improve how the manuscript reads as a whole.\n\nCopyediting works on grammar, punctuation, spelling, formatting and minor style, without changing the content. The practical consequence is sequencing: copyediting a section that development editing is going to cut means paying for the same words twice."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who benefits from development editing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Researchers preparing manuscripts for journals, authors writing books, theses or dissertations, and professionals preparing reports, grant proposals or technical documents.\n\nThe clearest case is a manuscript rejected on clarity or organisation. That objection is structural, and resubmitting a copyedited version of the same structure gets the same decision."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does a development editor look for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Logical flow and organisation of ideas; clarity and precision of language; the strength and consistency of arguments; adherence to journal or publisher guidelines; and redundancy, gaps or missing information.\n\nIn practice the first three findings are usually the same three: a framework arriving too late, a discussion that opens on caveats, and a conclusion that restates rather than concludes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will my manuscript be rewritten?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Editors suggest, reorganise for clarity and may rephrase sentences for flow, while preserving your voice and your ideas.\n\nStructural changes come as recommendations with reasons, and you accept or reject each one. An editor who reorganises a manuscript without asking has taken a decision that belongs to the author."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can development editing improve my chances of publication?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It makes the manuscript coherent, logically structured and clear about what it found, which is what reviewers and editors are reading for.\n\nNobody can guarantee acceptance, and be careful of anyone who says otherwise. What this changes is the part within your control — that the paper is not turned down for a reason that has nothing to do with the research."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you maintain confidentiality?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Every manuscript is handled under NDA, files are stored securely and access is limited to the assigned team. No third-party tools, and nothing is used for training.\n\nIf a confidentiality certificate is needed for your institution, ask and we will supply the current one rather than a claim about it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I talk to my editor?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, directly, to clarify a suggestion, discuss a structural recommendation or give context the manuscript does not carry. Most of the useful decisions in a development edit come out of that conversation.\n\nQuestions to the editor continue for as long as the paper is in review, not for a fixed window after delivery."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between development editing and peer review?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A development editor improves clarity, structure and flow so the manuscript is ready to be judged. A peer reviewer judges the scientific validity, the methodology and the significance of the research itself.\n\nThey are different questions, and an editor who starts answering the second one has exceeded what an edit can tell you. If what you want is the reviewer’s verdict early, that is pre-submission peer review."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Academic editorial","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/academic-editorial-services/development-editing-service/"}]}];

